<PhaseExecutionProtocol keywords="sdd, phase, worker, implementation, verification" type="directive" ver="2.3">
  <Mission>
    Implement exactly one approved ticket phase, verify it with declared real commands, and return a
    typed handoff. The worker never closes the phase or edits the ticket: `sdd-verify` owns only the
    receipt, while the orchestrator owns the later durable state transition. The worker is disposable;
    its conversation identity is never continuation state.
  </Mission>
  <BeliefState>


    <Axiom id="AX_TOOL_INVOCATION" cross-cutting="true">
      The SDD mechanical tools are `gennady` CLI commands. When working IN the gennady repo itself
      (including any worktree), the runner is `npx tsx <repo-root>/cli/gennady.ts`; in every other
      project it is `npx gennady`. Thus a Step's canonical `npx gennady <cmd> <args>` fixes the command
      and arguments exactly; substitute only this runner when developing gennady itself, using the
      current worktree's root. Tools: `sdd-state`, `sdd-task`, `sdd-extract`, `sdd-verify`, `sdd-log`,
      `sdd-sync`, `sdd-check`, `sdd-new`, `sdd-migrate`, `sdd-orient`, `orient`, `lint`, `yagni`, `testcov`. A
      tool result is the named context of its producing Step; later Steps use it by Step ID and field,
      without reconstructing it. When a Step gives an exact call, run it as written — no `--help` or
      argument guessing. Run one CLI operation per tool call; never pipe/truncate its verdict, suppress
      stderr, compose a shell loop, or inspect CLI source to reinterpret it. An unusable result is a named
      tool-contract blocker, not permission for `rg`/`cat` archaeology. The calling Step states the
      exact command and arguments next to the action, then names which returned field/output becomes
      the next action's input. A failed invocation is retried only after a new hypothesis changes the
      command or underlying state; failure is never hidden with `|| true`. The
      `/sdd-*` skills (`/sdd-execute`, `/sdd-reconcile`, `/sdd-critic`, `/sdd-scaffold`) are not CLI
      tools — never prefix those with the CLI runner.

      A validator verifies a completed mutation; it is not documentation or an authoring oracle.
      Never run `sdd-check` on a knowingly unfilled skeleton to discover what the manifest/format
      contract already states. Fill the artifact first, then run the owning Step's exact final check.
    </Axiom>


  </BeliefState>
  <Contracts></Contracts>
  <ExecutionPlan>
    - **STEP_1_ORIENT** — Consume only the supplied phase block, referenced spec/rule excerpts, target files, Execution Log facts, and Git evidence. Verify targets still match the ticket. Before executing this step, READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/phase-execution-protocol/steps/STEP_1_ORIENT.xml").
    - **STEP_2_IMPLEMENT** — Implement the smallest change satisfying the phase and its referenced requirements. Stay inside target files. Before executing this step, READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/phase-execution-protocol/steps/STEP_2_IMPLEMENT.xml").
    - **STEP_3_VERIFY** — Per `AX_VERIFICATION_BEFORE_HANDOFF`, run the phase's exact declared commands. Tests implementing a mandatory requirement include its Requirement-ID in the test source. Before executing this step, READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/phase-execution-protocol/steps/STEP_3_VERIFY.xml").
    - **STEP_4_HANDOFF** — Per `AX_HANDOFF_TYPED`, return `HANDOFF_FORMAT` with files changed, requirements demonstrated, commands/results, deviations, and the next declared phase. Return the payload in the response only. Before executing this step, READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/phase-execution-protocol/steps/STEP_4_HANDOFF.xml").
  </ExecutionPlan>
</PhaseExecutionProtocol>
